Kernir patch 0016: the 7zip and xar readers hold a whole header or TOC to an entry count and a growing names total

Upstream: libarchive 3.8.10, libarchive/archive_read.c (archive_read_new), archive_read_private.h (struct
archive_read), archive_read_support_format_7zip.c (read_PackInfo, read_CodersInfo, read_SubStreamsInfo, read_Header,
decode_encoded_header_info, slurp_central_directory), archive_read_support_format_xar.c (read_toc, file_new,
xattr_new, xml_data); include/kernir_names.h. Applies on top of 0001-0015 (0014 and 0015 lines included).

Both readers parse every entry before archive_read_next_header returns the first one, and nothing counted the
entries: a 763-byte 7z with 1,000,000 one-unit names in an LZMA-coded header cost 384 MiB and 8 s, and libarchive
allows 100,000,000 (UMAX_ENTRY), about 40 GB; the 7z folder, pack stream and unpack stream counts, read before
numFiles, had the same 100 M bound (128 bytes or more each). A xar <file> costs about 556 bytes while the TOC is
parsed, and xattr_new keeps a file's <ea> list sorted by walking it for each one: 20,000 <ea> on one file took
0.6 s, a million about 25 min.

kernir_read_set_name_limits(a, max_entries, names_bytes, name_bytes_per_entry), called before
archive_read_open, stores the limits in struct archive_read; archive_read_new sets the defaults
KERNIR_READ_MAX_ENTRIES (4,000,000), KERNIR_READ_NAMES_BYTES (256 MiB) and KERNIR_READ_NAME_BYTES_PER_ENTRY (512),
Kernir's NestingLimits.standard. kernir_read_get_name_limits reads them back. Then, before each allocation:
- 7zip: numFiles, numFolders and the unpack streams over max_entries, and pack streams over 4 × max_entries (a BCJ2
  folder has 4; saturated at UINT64_MAX), are refused ("7-Zip archive has more entries than the entry limit",
  "7-Zip archive has more folders or streams than the entry limit").
- 7zip: 0014's names block limit stays fixed: the block may hold 2 × names_bytes (512 MiB by default, 0014's
  KERNIR_7Z_NAMES_BYTES) plus 2 bytes per entry ("7-Zip entry names take more UTF-16 than the names limit"). It
  does not grow with numFiles, which the header only claims: grown by name_bytes_per_entry per claimed entry, an
  LZMA header of about 1 MB of zeros could have a 4.1 GB block malloc'd and filled before the first header, a
  jetsam kill on iOS and in Quick Look instead of a refusal.
- xar: the <file> elements (bare folders included) over max_entries, the <ea> elements over max_entries, and more
  than KERNIR_XAR_EAS_PER_FILE (1,024) <ea> on one <file> are refused ("xar archive has more entries than the
  entry limit", "xar archive has more extended attributes than the entry limit", "xar entry has more than 1024
  extended attributes").
- xar: 0015's names total grows: max(names_bytes, name_bytes_per_entry × the <file> elements so far), for the
  hardlink copies with every <file> ("xar entry names and link targets take more than the names limit", "xar
  hardlink targets take the entry names past the names limit").
These match what the caller lists: Kernir counts every header and grows its names total the same way, so nothing it
would list is refused, except xar's bare folders, which count here and are never listed, and 7z names over 512 MiB
of UTF-16 in an archive of more than 524,288 entries. Every refusal sets
ARCHIVE_ERRNO_FILE_FORMAT with its own message and returns ARCHIVE_FATAL; a 7zip refusal deep in the streams info
sets zip->kernir_refused, so neither "Damaged" nor "Malformed 7-Zip archive" replaces the message. Archives within
the limits read as before.

